<header class="page-header">
  <p class="page-header__eyebrow">Topic</p>
  <h1 class="page-header__title">Mycoremediation</h1>
  <p class="page-header__lead">
    Mycoremediation is a branch of bioremediation that uses fungi
    and their enzymes to break down pollutants. This page collects
    materials on the key mechanisms, studies and practical results.
  </p>
</header>

<section>
  <h2>What it is</h2>
  <p>
    The term "mycoremediation" describes the use of fungi — most
    often mycelium — to destroy or bind pollutants: petroleum
    products, pesticides, heavy metals, some plastics and
    synthetic dyes. The key idea is that the fungi's extracellular
    enzymes (laccases, peroxidases, cytochromes P450) can cleave
    complex organic molecules that are beyond bacteria's reach.
  </p>

  <h2>Main directions</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Mycodegradation</strong> — breaking down organic pollutants (oil, PAHs, pesticides).</li>
    <li><strong>Mycosorption</strong> — binding heavy metals and radionuclides in fungal biomass.</li>
    <li><strong>Mycofiltration</strong> — passing contaminated water through mycelial mats.</li>
    <li><strong>Mycostabilization</strong> — locking a pollutant into a matrix that prevents its spread.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Key research results</h2>
  <p>
    Among the most cited work are Paul Stamets's publications on
    petroleum degradation and on using mycelium for water
    filtration in ecosystems after spills. Independent laboratories
    have confirmed the ability of a number of species to degrade
    components of crude oil and some organochlorine pesticides.
  </p>
  <p>
    There are also data showing that certain fungi can accumulate
    cadmium, lead and arsenic — used in experimental soil-cleanup
    schemes around former industrial zones.
  </p>
</section>

